From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ryan Murray <ryan@aurorasilicon.org>
Date: Mon, 2 Nov 2026 17:06:17 +1100
Subject: [PATCH] media: apple: ProRes and JPEG performance requests on T8140

At 3840x2160 the Neo encoded ProRes at about 60% and JPEG at about 85%
of macOS on the same machine, because nothing asked the PMP to raise
the SoC rail while the engines worked. macOS holds a VMAX request for
each engine while it encodes.

Describe the two PMP report entries, ProRes (soc-device record 14,
SOC-DEV-DVFS entry 0x197) and the JPEG pair (record 19, entry 0x19c),
both at level 2, and give them to the engines as their "perf" power
domain next to the physical one. Both drivers attach the domain list
with runtime PM links, so the request is held exactly while an engine is
runtime-active, as AVD and AVE already do. The DARTs keep only their
physical domains.

Assisted-by: LLM
Signed-off-by: Ryan Murray <ryan@aurorasilicon.org>
(backported from commit a02f819f0545afd009c88da99f7acd563d122721)
[j604: JPEG hunks only (apple,jpeg.yaml, apple-jpeg-drv.c): the ProRes driver and binding and the t8140.dtsi PMP entries are not in this series; the driver code attaches a second "perf" domain only when the node lists one, and the J604 nodes list only ps_jpg]
Signed-off-by: Dylan Ironside <dylan@dylanironside.io>
---
 .../devicetree/bindings/media/apple,jpeg.yaml | 11 ++++++++++-
 .../platform/apple/jpeg/apple-jpeg-drv.c      | 19 ++++++++++++++++++-
 2 files changed, 28 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/Documentation/devicetree/bindings/media/apple,jpeg.yaml b/Documentation/devicetree/bindings/media/apple,jpeg.yaml
index 839d29392c..056e1d33c0 100644
--- a/Documentation/devicetree/bindings/media/apple,jpeg.yaml
+++ b/Documentation/devicetree/bindings/media/apple,jpeg.yaml
@@ -35,7 +35,16 @@ properties:
     maxItems: 1
 
   power-domains:
-    maxItems: 1
+    minItems: 1
+    items:
+      - description: The engine's power domain
+      - description: Performance request held while the engine works
+
+  power-domain-names:
+    minItems: 1
+    items:
+      - const: core
+      - const: perf
 
   apple,dma-range:
     $ref: /schemas/types.yaml#/definitions/uint64-array
diff --git a/drivers/media/platform/apple/jpeg/apple-jpeg-drv.c b/drivers/media/platform/apple/jpeg/apple-jpeg-drv.c
index d8b2b2141b..736ffbeba5 100644
--- a/drivers/media/platform/apple/jpeg/apple-jpeg-drv.c
+++ b/drivers/media/platform/apple/jpeg/apple-jpeg-drv.c
@@ -16,6 +16,7 @@
 #include <linux/mutex.h>
 #include <linux/of.h>
 #include <linux/platform_device.h>
+#include <linux/pm_domain.h>
 #include <linux/pm_runtime.h>
 #include <linux/slab.h>
 #include <linux/spinlock.h>
@@ -40,6 +41,7 @@ struct apple_jpeg {
 	struct device *dev;
 	void __iomem *regs;
 	const struct apple_jpeg_variant *variant;
+	struct dev_pm_domain_list *pd_list;
 	struct mutex lock;	/* serialises ioctls and queue state */
 	struct v4l2_device v4l2_dev;
 	struct video_device vdev;
@@ -718,8 +720,23 @@ static int aj_probe(struct platform_device *pdev)
 
 	if (!device_iommu_mapped(dev))
 		return dev_err_probe(dev, -ENODEV, "no IOMMU\n");
-	if (!dev->pm_domain)
+	/*
+	 * A second power domain, "perf", is the SoC performance request the
+	 * engine needs for full speed; attach both with runtime PM links so it
+	 * is held exactly while the engine is runtime-active.
+	 */
+	if (of_count_phandle_with_args(dev->of_node, "power-domains",
+				       "#power-domain-cells") > 1) {
+		static const struct dev_pm_domain_attach_data pd_data = {
+			.pd_flags = PD_FLAG_DEV_LINK_ON,
+		};
+
+		ret = devm_pm_domain_attach_list(dev, &pd_data, &jpeg->pd_list);
+		if (ret < 0)
+			return dev_err_probe(dev, ret, "failed to attach power domains\n");
+	} else if (!dev->pm_domain) {
 		return dev_err_probe(dev, -ENODEV, "requires a power domain\n");
+	}
 	res = platform_get_resource(pdev, IORESOURCE_MEM, 0);
 	if (!res || resource_size(res) != APPLE_JPEG_REG_SIZE)
 		return -EINVAL;
